  "id": "108833",
  "subject": [
    "dare:major=0",
    "dare:ancient=0",
    "dare:feature=temple"
  ],
  "title": "Gallo-Roman Sanctuary at B\u00fb",
  "provenance": "Barrington Atlas: BAtlas 11 B4 B\u00fb",
  "placeTypeURIs": [
    "https://pleiades.stoa.org/vocabularies/place-types/sanctuary"
  ],
  "details": "<p>This site is located in a section of <a class=\"external-link\" href=\"https://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/For%C3%AAt_de_Dreux\" target=\"_self\" title=\"\">the For\u00eat domaniale de Dreux</a> directly north of B\u00fb.</p>\r\n<p>The site name is given variously in the literature and online. Charles-Picard wrote in the 1960s that the ancient site was \"d\u00e9sign\u00e9 dans le cadastre sous le nom de <em>pi\u00e8ce des fours \u00e0 chaux</em>\" and was locally known as <em>pi\u00e8ce de l'\u00e9glise</em>. Writing a decade later, Kisch lists it under the rubric \"B\u00fb - <em>Au Bois du Four \u00e0 Chaux</em>.\" Fauduet, writing in the 1980s, prefers the fuller descriptive title \"<em>Le sanctuaire Gallo-romain des Bois du Four \u00e0 Chaux \u00e0 B\u00fb.</em>\" The <em>Barrington Atlas</em> followed its standard practice of labeling sites whose ancient name is unknown simply with the name of nearest modern settlement or administrative district (i.e., \"B\u00fb\").</p>\r\n<p>The original excavations of the 1960s seem never to have been published (so Fauduet 1990, page 93, note 1), but new excavations in the 1980s resulted in an exhibition at the municipal museum in Dreux (catalogue: Fauduet 1984) and a trio of reports in the <em>Bulletin de la Soci\u00e9t\u00e9 arch\u00e9ologique d'Eure-et-Loir</em> (Fauduet 1988, 1988a, 1988b).</p>",
  "review_state": "published",
  "type": "FeatureCollection",
  "description": "Remains of a rectangular Romano-Celtic temple were excavated north of the French town of B\u00fb (in the department of Eure-et-Loir), beginning in the 1960s. Other buildings of uncertain nature and a theater were also identified in the vicinity. Coins and pottery discovered at the site indicate there was activity there during the Roman imperial period.",
